"Proof of Stahl’s conjecture in some new cases - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X20302079)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Saul Stahl defined the s-tuple coloring of a graph G and formulated a conjecture on the multichromatic numbers of the Kneser graph K{G}_{m,n}. ... This was the famous Kneser conjecture proved by László Lovász [2].\n\n## Discrete Applied Mathematics\n\nVolume 285, 15 October 2020, Pages 113-118\n\nNote\n\nProof of Stahl’s conjecture in some new cases\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2020.04.025Get rights and content\n\nUnder an Elsevier user license\n\n## Abstract\n\nSaul Stahl defined the s-tuple coloring of a graph G and formulated a conjecture on the multichromatic numbers of the Kneser graph K{G}_{m,n}. The conjecture was completely proved for graph parameters n=2,3, and m=2n+1 by Stahl, and in specially for m=10 and n=4 by Kincses et al. Furthermore we know that the conjecture is true if s=qn. In this paper we present a new lower bound for the multichromatic numbers of K{G}_{m,n}, which is sharp for graph parameters 2n<m<3n and cases qn-\\frac{n}{m-2n}<s<qn. So we confirm Stahl’s conjecture in these new cases.\n\n\n## Keywords\n\nMultichromatic number\n\nOrthoposet\n\nKneser graph\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nFor any positive integers t and s, an s-tuple coloring of a graph G with t colors is an assignment of s distinct colors to each vertex of G in such a way that no two adjacent vertices share a color. {\\chi }_{s}\\left(G\\right), the s th multichromatic number of G, is the least integer t such that G has an s-tuple coloring with t colors. We note that the usual chromatic number of G coincides with {\\chi }_{1}\\left(G\\right).\n\nThe multichromatic numbers of graphs were first studied by Stahl [4] in the early 70s. He cleared the central role of Kneser graphs in these colorings. For positive integers m\\ge 2n, the Kneser graph K{G}_{m,n} is defined as the graph with vertices of all n-subsets of \\left[m\\right]=\\{1,2,\\dots ,m\\}, and with edges connecting disjoint pairs of n-subsets. An n-tuple coloring of G with m colors is a graph homomorphism from G to K{G}_{m,n}. Stahl formulated the following conjecture on the multichromatic numbers of K{G}_{m,n}.\n\nConjecture 1\n\n## Stahl, [4]\n\nFor all 2n\\le m and q, 0\\le r<n {\\chi }_{nq-r}\\left(K{G}_{m,n}\\right)=mq-2r.\n\nIn the case of nq-r=1 the conjecture is true. This was the famous Kneser conjecture proved by László Lovász [2]. Stahl showed that {\\chi }_{nq}\\left(K{G}_{m,n}\\right)=mq for all positive integers 2n\\le m and q, and also that (1){\\chi }_{s+1}\\ge {\\chi }_{s}+2 for any positive integer s. This confirmed the conjecture for 1<s\\le n, that is {\\chi }_{s}\\left(K{G}_{m,n}\\right)={\\chi }_{s-1}\\left(K{G}_{m,n}\\right)+2=m-2n+2s\\phantom{\\rule{1em}{0ex}}\\text{ for }1<s\\le n.\n\nThe multichromatic numbers of K{G}_{m,n} are trivial for n=1 and Stahl computed them for n=2,3, and m=2n+1 in [4], [5]. Next, the conjecture was proved for special parameters (m=10 and n=4) in [1]. In the papers [3], [5], the authors studied the gap between the multichromatic numbers {\\chi }_{nq}\\left(K{G}_{m,n}\\right) and {\\chi }_{nq+1}\\left(K{G}_{m,n}\\right). Namely, the inequality {\\chi }_{nq}\\left(K{G}_{m,n}\\right)+{\\chi }_{1}\\left(K{G}_{m,n}\\right)\\le {\\chi }_{nq+1}\\left(K{G}_{m,n}\\right),would imply the conjecture. The most significant result was proved by Stahl in [5]: {\\chi }_{nq}\\left(K{G}_{m,n}\\right)+{\\chi }_{1}\\left(K{G}_{m,n}\\right)-f\\left(n\\right)\\le {\\chi }_{nq+1}\\left(K{G}_{m,n}\\right),where f\\left(n\\right)={n}^{2}-3n+4.\n\nIn this paper we focus on the cases where the conjectured jump is 2. We shall give a new lower bound for the multichromatic numbers of K{G}_{m,n}, which turns out to be sharp for an interval of values preceding nq. So we confirm Conjecture 1 for graph parameters 2n<m<3n and cases 0\\le r<\\frac{n}{m-2n}.\n\nProposition 2\n\nFor any positive integers n,q and 2n<m<3n, 0\\le r<\\frac{n}{m-2n} {\\chi }_{nq-r}\\left(K{G}_{m,n}\\right)=mq-2r.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe kite graph is determined by its adjacency spectrum - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0096300318301474)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.3 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Also, Stani\\stackrel{`}{c}$\\stackrel{`}{c}$ [20] has proved that among connected graphs any double kite graph is determined by its adjacency spectrum.\n\n## Applied Mathematics and Computation\n\nVolume 330, 1 August 2018, Pages 134-142\n\n# The kite graph is determined by its adjacency spectrum\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.amc.2018.02.037Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe kite graph is obtained by appending the complete graph with p vertices to a pendant vertex of the path graph with q vertices. In this paper, we prove that the kite graph is determined by its adjacency spectrum for all p and q.\n\n## Introduction\n\nIn this paper, unless indicated otherwise, all graphs are simple and undirected. Let G=\\left(V\\right(G),E(G\\left)\\right)$G=\\left(V\\right(G),E(G\\left)\\right)$ be a graph with vertex set V\\left(G\\right)=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},\\dots ,{v}_{n}\\}$V\\left(G\\right)=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},\\dots ,{v}_{n}\\}$ and edge set E(G). As usual, P_{n}, C_{n} and K_{n} denotes the path, the cycle and the complete graph with n vertices, respectively. The order of G is the number of vertices in G and it is denoted by |G|. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKite graphs determined by their spectra - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0096300316306397)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.6 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; In Section 3, prove that the kite graph K{i}_{n,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}n-1}$K{i}_{n,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}n-1}$ is determined by its signless Laplacian spectrum when n ≠ 5 and n ≥ 4, and K{i}_{n,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}n-1}$K{i}_{n,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}n-1}$ is also determined by its distance spectrum when n ≥ 4.\n\n## Applied Mathematics and Computation\n\nVolume 297, 15 March 2017, Pages 74-78\n\n# Kite graphs determined by their spectra\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.amc.2016.10.032Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nA kite graph Ki_{n, ω} is a graph obtained from a clique K_{ω} and a path {P}_{n-\\omega }${P}_{n-\\omega }$ by adding an edge between a vertex from the clique and an endpoint from the path. In this note, we prove that K{i}_{n,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}n-1}$K{i}_{n,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}n-1}$ is determined by its signless Laplacian spectrum when n ≠ 5 and n ≥ 4, and K{i}_{n,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}n-1}$K{i}_{n,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}n-1}$ is also determined by its distance spectrum when n ≥ 4.\n\n## Introduction\n\nThroughout this paper, G=(V,E)$G=(V,E)$ is a simple and undirected graph with vertex set V\\left(G\\right)=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},\\dots ,{v}_{n}\\}$V\\left(G\\right)=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},\\dots ,{v}_{n}\\}$ and edge set E(G). If G and H are isomorphic, they are necessarily cospectral. A graph G is said to be determined by its signless Laplacian spectrum (simplified as Q-DS$Q-DS$) if there is no other non-isomorphic graph with the same signless Laplacian spectrum as G, that is, {S}_{Q}\\left(H\\right)={S}_{Q}\\left(G\\right)${S}_{Q}\\left(H\\right)={S}_{Q}\\left(G\\right)$ implies H ≅ G for any graph H. Similarly, we can define the definitions of “determined by its distance spectrum” (simplified as DI-DS$DI-DS$), “determined by its adjacency spectrum” (simplified as A-DS$A-DS$) and “determined by its Laplacian spectrum” (simplified as L-DS$L-DS$). The problem “which graphs are determined by their spectrum?” was posed by Günthard and Primas [5] more than 50 years ago in the context of Hückel’s theory in chemistry. Only in the most recent years have mathematicians devoted their attention to this problem and many papers focus on this topic are now appearing. For recent results in this topic, see [3], [4], [7], [8], [10].\n\nA kite graph Ki_{n, ω} is a graph obtained from a clique K_{ω} and a path {P}_{n-\\omega }${P}_{n-\\omega }$ by adding an edge between a vertex of K_{ω} and an endpoint of {P}_{n-\\omega }${P}_{n-\\omega }$. Recently, the research involving the graph spectrum of kite graphs attains much attention. For instance, the properties of adjacency spectrum, signless Laplacian spectrum and distance spectrum of kite graphs have been studied in [1], [12], [14], [15], [17]. Furthermore, it has been proved that no two non-isomorphic kite graphs are cospectral with respect to adjacency matrix [13], all K{i}_{\\omega +2,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}\\omega }$K{i}_{\\omega +2,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}\\omega }$ are A-DS$A-DS$ [13], and all K{i}_{\\omega +1,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}\\omega }$K{i}_{\\omega +1,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}\\omega }$ are L-DS$L-DS$ (see Corollary 2.1 [10]). Motivated from these results, we shall discuss the spectral characterization of kite graphs in this paper.\n\nThis paper is organized as follows. In Section 2, we give a list of some previously known results. In Section 3, prove that the kite graph K{i}_{n,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}n-1}$K{i}_{n,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}n-1}$ is determined by its signless Laplacian spectrum when n ≠ 5 and n ≥ 4, and K{i}_{n,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}n-1}$K{i}_{n,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}n-1}$ is also determined by its distance spectrum when n ≥ 4.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQ:\\ACTAPROB\\ACTA7412.DVI (https://acta.hu/download.phtml?id=2993)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.3 years ago; Further Stahl conﬁrmed his conjecture for the case s = nq in [3] by showing ... Lov´asz’s 